    "n": 2,
    "mode": "native",
    "native": "<div class=\"slide-native sm-skill sm-check\" style=\"--accent:var(--aws-blue);\" data-correct=\"B\" data-multi=\"\" data-rationale=\"ElastiCache provides an in-memory data store that enables sub-millisecond latency for session data. All instances can access the shared session store. This decouples session state from individual instances and provides the lowest latency option for session management.\" data-judge=\"{&quot;prompt&quot;: &quot;Question 1: Session state across instances&quot;, &quot;options&quot;: [&quot;Store session data in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Configure the application to read and write session data to DynamoDB.&quot;, &quot;Store session data in Amazon ElastiCache. Configure the application to read and write session data to the ElastiCache cluster.&quot;, &quot;Enable sticky sessions on the ALB target group. Configure the session cookie duration to match the application timeout.&quot;, &quot;Store session data in an Amazon S3 bucket. Configure the application to read and write session data to S3 on each request.&quot;], &quot;answer&quot;: &quot;Store session data in Amazon ElastiCache. Configure the application to read and write session data to the ElastiCache cluster.&quot;, &quot;why&quot;: &quot;ElastiCache provides an in-memory data store that enables sub-millisecond latency for session data. All instances can access the shared session store. This decouples session state from individual instances and provides the lowest latency option for session management.&quot;, &quot;explanations&quot;: {&quot;A&quot;: &quot;DynamoDB can store session data durably. However, DynamoDB has higher read latency compared to an in-memory data store. For session management where latency is critical, an in-memory store is more appropriate.&quot;, &quot;B&quot;: &quot;ElastiCache provides an in-memory data store that enables sub-millisecond latency for session data. All instances can access the shared session store. This decouples session state from individual instances and provides the lowest latency option for session management.&quot;, &quot;C&quot;: &quot;Sticky sessions bind a user to a specific instance. This does not maintain state across instances. If the bound instance becomes unhealthy or is terminated during scaling events, the session is still lost. This approach does not meet the requirement to maintain session state across all instances.&quot;, &quot;D&quot;: &quot;Amazon S3 is a durable object store but has higher latency compared to an in-memory data store. S3 is not designed for frequent small read/write operations typical of session management. The per-request latency would not meet the requirement for least latency.&quot;}, &quot;sources&quot;: [&quot;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/elasticache-use-cases.html&quot;]}\" data-sources=\"[&quot;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/elasticache-use-cases.html&quot;]\"><div class=\"kicker\">SESSION 1 &middot; KNOWLEDGE CHECK &middot; QUESTION 1 of 5</div><h2>Check yourself <span class=\"sm-check-hint\">click an option</span></h2><p class=\"sm-check-q\">Question 1: Session state across instances</p><div class=\"sm-opts\"><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"A\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">A</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Store session data in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Configure the application to read and write session data to DynamoDB.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"B\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">B</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Store session data in Amazon ElastiCache. Configure the application to read and write session data to the ElastiCache cluster.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"C\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">C</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Enable sticky sessions on the ALB target group. Configure the session cookie duration to match the application timeout.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"D\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">D</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Store session data in an Amazon S3 bucket. Configure the application to read and write session data to S3 on each request.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button></div><div class=\"sm-check-fb\" style=\"display:none\"></div><div class=\"sm-source\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-circle-question\"></i> Knowledge check &middot; Content Review Session 1</div></div>",
